{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And these are the generations of Aaron and Moses, etc.</b> — It lists only the sons of Aaron, yet they are called the generations of Moses because he taught them Torah, teaching that whoever teaches his fellow Torah, Scripture accounts it as though he gave birth to him. <b>On the day YHWH spoke to Moses</b> — these became his generations, for he taught them what he had learned from the mouth of the Almighty. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 3:1). And the phrase \"on the day YHWH spoke to Moses on Mount Sinai\" means only to say that these were their generations on the day HaShem spoke to Moses on Mount Sinai, and it states that Nadav and Avihu died and they have no generations today except Elazar and Itamar. And it mentioned this to say that on the day YHWH spoke to Moses on Mount Sinai, these were chosen to be anointed and to have their anointing as an everlasting priesthood, while the rest of the tribe was not commanded regarding their selection until now. And the reason it mentioned \"the generations of Aaron and Moses\" in this place is that once it completed the counting of all Israel by their generations to their ancestral houses and wished to mention the generations of the tribe of Levi, it began with the heads of that tribe. And according to the plain sense, the meaning of \"the generations of Aaron and Moses\" is that the sons of Aaron were anointed priests, set apart from the tribe as most holy, and the generations of Moses are the family of the Amramite, which it mentions below (Numbers 3:27), for among the Amramite none are counted among the Levites except the sons of Moses. This is like the matter that is written (1 Chronicles 23:13–14): \"The sons of Amram: Aaron and Moses, and Aaron was set apart to consecrate him as most holy, and Moses the man of Elohim — his sons are called over the tribe of Levi.\" And the derash relies upon it because it did not specify \"and these are the names of the sons of Moses\" as it did for the sons of Aaron, to hint that the sons of Aaron are also generations of Moses because he taught them Torah, for the Torah will expound and hint:"], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :register {:flags {0 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {0 "The letter ה׳ in the phrase 'ביום דבר ה״את משה' renders the divine name as YHWH per voice rules; in the following phrase 'דבר השם' it renders as HaShem."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:02:23.995238307Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}